<p><strong>Spa management software runs a spa or wellness centre from one system: online booking, therapist and room scheduling, payments, discounts and reports. The thing that separates it from a general calendar is that it books the therapist, the room and the time together, so none is double-booked. This page explains what it is and how a day runs on it &mdash; to compare products, read the <a href="/blog/best-spa-software-nepal/">spa software buyer's guide</a>.</strong></p>

<h2>What makes a spa different</h2>
<p>A treatment needs a person and a room for a set length of time. Three things have to line up, not one, and that is the whole reason generic booking tools struggle here. Book only the therapist and two guests end up assigned the same room. Book only the room and a therapist is promised to two places at once. Spa software books therapist, room and time as a single booking and refuses the clash, so a confirmation means something.</p>
<p>The second difference is that treatment lengths genuinely vary &mdash; a 30-minute foot massage and a 120-minute package are not interchangeable blocks &mdash; and each one needs turnaround time for cleaning and resetting the room. Leave that out and the schedule looks correct on screen while running twenty minutes late by noon.</p>

<h2>A spa day, from opening to close</h2>

<h3>Opening</h3>
<p>The front desk opens the day and sees two things side by side: which therapists are in, on leave or covering at another branch, and which rooms are free when. Therapists mark themselves present. Nothing about the day depends on the owner being reachable by phone.</p>

<h3>During the day</h3>
<p>Guests book online, choosing a treatment and seeing only slots where a therapist and a suitable room are both genuinely free. Walk-in guests and hotel referrals are added by the desk the same way. Because the clash is refused at the point of booking, the failure mode that annoys guests most &mdash; arriving to be told the room is taken &mdash; largely disappears. <a href="/blog/two-customers-one-room-double-booking-nepal/">Two guests, one room</a> walks through how that used to happen.</p>
<p>As each guest leaves, the payment is recorded against the booking by method: cash, card or online, with part-payments recorded as part-payments. Memberships, packages and vouchers are redeemed against their balance rather than noted in a book. Any discount above a staff member's limit goes to a manager and the approval is stored with the booking.</p>

<h3>Closing</h3>
<p>The daily close compares booked, delivered and collected per branch. Done this way it is a check, not a reconstruction &mdash; and when something does not agree, the booking responsible is named rather than hunted for. <a href="/blog/cash-short-at-closing-spa-salon-nepal/">Cash short at closing</a> covers what the gaps usually turn out to be.</p>

<h2>What it adds up to across the month</h2>
<p>Revenue by branch and by treatment, which therapists are busiest and which earn most, discounts given and who approved them, and the hours each therapist actually worked &mdash; which is what <a href="/features/payroll/">commission and payroll</a> are calculated from. None of this needs a separate exercise at month end; it is the same records the daily close already used.</p>

<h2>Spas with more than one location</h2>
<p>A second spa location adds new problems: therapists who work at both, price differences and an owner who cannot see every site at once. Look for software that schedules staff transfers in advance and gives one dashboard for all locations. See <a href="/blog/multi-location-booking-software/">multi-location booking software</a> and our guide to <a href="/blog/how-to-manage-a-spa-in-nepal/">managing a spa</a>.</p>

<h2>What it does not do</h2>
<p>Zennly records what was charged and how it was paid against each booking; it is not a POS and does not issue tax invoices. Guest outreach is by email as standard, with SMS and WhatsApp available on request rather than on by default. There is no Khalti or eSewa integration and no open API today.</p>

<h2>Where Zennly fits</h2>
<p>Zennly is built for multi-branch spas, with therapist and room booking, discount approval, payment tracking and a daily close. Nuad Thai Spa Kathmandu runs four branches on it. If you run a single treatment room, a simpler calendar may be enough. See <a href="/solutions/spa/">how Zennly works for spas</a> and the <a href="/case-study/">Nuad Thai case study</a>.</p>
